# The dense layer moved to the end: dense0.bux (the factorial's candidate with every
# layer's FFN routed) with the last layer's FFN the candidate's dense SwiGLU, so that
# it has one dense layer, as the candidate does, at the other end. A probe, from no
# model: does a dense layer help where the routing is the most specialized, and does
# the gradient spike there too, as the candidate's dense first layer makes it do? The
# MTP module keeps its layer of experts. Everything else is dense0's and the
# candidate's, so it pairs with both by seed.
use m2026_hybrid
use timeline_code
use rungs

# factorial.bux's vres(g_k, fr_experts), every FFN routed but the last layer's.
g_k = fr_global(kv_conv_size=4)

# The groups of four after the first, layers // 4 - 1 of them, the last with the dense
# FFN. A repeat cannot run 0 times, as the others would at d512: each rung has its own.
group = fr_layer(hy_delta)^3 fr_layer(g_k(v=$v1))
last  = fr_layer(hy_delta)^3 fr_layer(g_k(v=$v1), fr_dense)
tail  = d512: last | d768: group last | d1024: group^2 last | d1280: group^3 last

dense_last = embed
             fr_layer(hy_delta) fr_layer(hy_delta)^2 fr_layer(g_k(v=@v1))
             tail
             fr_norm @h fr_out hy_mtp
model      = dense_last

residual = hc(expansion=4)

# The hybrid's best at d512 (milestones.bux's scan), as the candidate's.
optimizer.lr = 2e-3
